                "senderName": "NWS Tallahassee FL",
                "headline": "Flood Watch issued October 4 at 2:09AM EDT until October 5 at 8:00AM EDT by NWS Tallahassee FL",
                "description": "* WHAT...Flash flooding caused by excessive rainfall continues to be\npossible.\n\n* WHERE...Portions of southeast Alabama, including the following\nareas, Geneva and Houston, Florida, including the following areas,\nCalhoun, Coastal Franklin, Coastal Jefferson, Coastal Wakulla,\nGadsden, Inland Franklin, Inland Jefferson, Inland Wakulla,\nJackson, Leon, Madison, Northern Liberty and Southern Liberty, and\nGeorgia, including the following areas, Baker, Ben Hill, Berrien,\nBrooks, Calhoun, Colquitt, Cook, Decatur, Dougherty, Early, Grady,\nIrwin, Lanier, Lee, Lowndes, Miller, Mitchell, Randolph, Seminole,\nTerrell, Thomas, Tift, Turner and Worth.\n\n* WHEN...Through Monday morning.\n\n* IMPACTS...Excessive runoff may result in flooding of rivers,\ncreeks, streams, and other low-lying and flood-prone locations.\nFlooding may occur in poor drainage and urban areas.\n\n* ADDITIONAL DETAILS...\n- Additional rainfall totals of 2 to 6 inches are forecast for most\nof the area with locally higher amounts of up to 10 inches\npossible, especially along the Emerald Coast. Another area of\nconcern is across south-central Georgia where locally heavy rain\nthe past two afternoons has primed the region more so than the\nrest of our area, so it wont take as much rain to cause flash\nflooding. Additionally, very high rain rates due to the tropical\nair mass in place may lead to flash flooding, especially in urban\nand poor drainage areas.\n- http://www.weather.gov/safety/flood",
                "instruction": "You should monitor later forecasts and be prepared to take action\nshould Flash Flood Warnings be issued.",
                "response": "Prepare",
                "note": null,
